              text: "Everybody who grew up in a street like ours knows what an [[entry|el callejón de detrás de las casas]] is, and everybody who didn't has to have it explained. It's the narrow lane that runs behind the houses, between the back yards. It's where the [[wheelie bins|cubos con ruedas]] live, where the coal used to come in, and where children learned to ride a bike, because there were never any cars in it.<br/><br/>Ours hasn't been used for bikes in a long time. Last winter somebody left a mattress in it. In February there were three bags of rubble, which is [[fly-tipping|tirar escombros donde no se debe]] and carries a fine, and in March there was litter the whole length of it after the bags split open. To be fair, the council did come and take the mattress away. Having said that, the rubble is still there.<br/><br/>At the March meeting somebody suggested putting a [[gate|verja]] at each end, with a key for every house. Fifteen people came, which is the best turnout we have ever had.<br/><br/>Marie McCrea, who has lived at number 24 since 1979, spoke first and spoke in favour. \"I remember when this entry was full of children,\" she said. \"Now I don't walk down it at all after four o'clock, and I'm not the only one. I've stopped putting my bin out at night. I do it in the morning, because I don't want to be out there in the dark.\"<br/><br/>Not everybody agreed, and the objections were practical rather than personal. The bin lorry can't get down a gated entry, so the bins would have to be brought round to the front. Two people said that would be hard for older residents, and one asked the question nobody had an answer to: who keeps the spare key, and what happens at two in the morning when somebody has lost theirs?<br/><br/>Then there is the matter of who actually owns the entry, and this is where people from other parts of the United Kingdom get caught out. Councils here don't do the same jobs they do in England. Bins, litter fines, parks and dog fouling are ours; the roads are not, and never have been — they belong to the Department for Infrastructure. So the man who rang the council four times about the state of the surface was ringing the wrong number four times, politely, for three weeks. And some entries are [[unadopted|que ninguna administración ha asumido]], which means in practice that they belong to the houses on either side and nobody has ever written it down.<br/><br/>The meeting didn't end in a vote. It ended in something better. Marie proposed the gates; the people worried about the bins didn't propose anything at all; and then a man at the back who hadn't said a word all evening asked, \"What if we tried it for six months and kept a note of everything that goes wrong?\"<br/><br/>That is what we're doing. The application goes in this month, the gates would go up in the spring, and there is a sheet on the noticeboard in the community centre for anybody who wants to write down a wee problem before it turns into a big one.<br/><br/>Come and put your name on it, even if you're against. Especially if you're against.",
